title: Undo and redo
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: guide
aliases: undo redo
summary: mf log undo (Ctrl+Z) takes back the newest change you made, whatever the watcher wrote since; mf log redo (Ctrl+Shift+Z) takes the newest undo back.

```
mf -n music log undo plan      # what undo would do, and why — nothing written
mf -n music log undo
mf -n music log redo plan
mf -n music log redo
```

In the GUI: <<key "log:undo" Ctrl+z>> and <<key "log:redo" Ctrl+Shift+z>>, from anywhere.

''Undo'' takes back the newest change ''you'' made that still stands — not the watcher's,
not a previous undo — by reverting it ([[The undo selection]]): the watcher's revisions
written since stay where they are — they record what really happened on disk ([[Why undo does not step HEAD back]]). Pressed again, it takes back the change before, and so on.
The undo is itself a revision in the log.

''Redo'' takes the newest undo that still stands back, by reverting it — whatever the
watcher wrote in between. Pressed again, it takes back the undo before. A new change of
yours clears what could be redone; it re-applies undos only — not arbitrary history (that
is `mf log revert`). Undo and redo compose: undo, redo, undo lands where the first undo
did ([[Why undo and redo do not oscillate]]).

Neither needs a target: finding it is their whole job. `plan` shows the revision and the
reason, and writes nothing. With nothing to take back they say `Nothing to undo.` /
`Nothing to redo.` and exit 0. `mf log undo --after <op>` undoes again and again while your
newest change is newer than that operation — what a script uses to go back to where it was
(`mf log head` gives the operation; [[Writing a GUI script]]).

Files come along. Undoing a trashing brings the file back from the trash, redoing it
sends it there again; undoing a move made through metafolder moves the file back
([[Trash, undo and redo]]). On the command line the move policies apply
(`--on-move-available`, default `apply`; `--on-move-unavailable`, default `ask`), with
`--metadata-only`, `--force`, `--silent` passed to the revert. Undo and redo do not ask
before moving files — `mf log revert` does —; they ask before overwriting a later write to
the same field, which `--force` skips ([[Reverting onto the current state]]). The GUI asks
nothing: a move is applied if the file is there, and applied to the metadata alone if it
is not.
